"[2511.09354v1] Spider4SSC & S2CLite: A text-to-multi-query-language dataset using lightweight ontology-agnostic SPARQL to Cypher parser (https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.09354v1)\nciteturn5953view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 weeks ago; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.09354v1\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 159\nL0: cite0†Search cite1†Submit cite2†Donate†info.arxiv.org cite3†Log in L1: \nL2: Search arXiv [Input: Search papers by title, author, abstract, or ID...] [Input] [Input]\nL3: \nL4: Press Enter to search · cite4†Advanced search L5: \nL6: # Computer Science > Computation and Language\nL7: \nL8: [Submitted on 12 Nov 2025]\nL9: # Title:Spider4SSC & S2CLite: A text-to-multi-query-language dataset using lightweight ontology-agnostic SPARQL to Cypher parser\nL10: \nL11: Authors:cite5†Martin Vejvar , cite6†Yasutaka Fujimoto L12: \nL13: View a PDF of the paper titled Spider4SSC & S2CLite: A text-to-multi-query-language dataset using lightweight ontology-agnostic SPARQL to Cypher parser, by Martin Vejvar and Yasutaka Fujimoto\nL14: \nL15: cite7†View PDF cite8†HTML (experimental) L16: > Abstract:We present Spider4SSC dataset and S2CLite parsing tool. S2CLite is a lightweight, ontology-agnostic parser that translates SPARQL queries into Cypher queries, enabling both in-situ and large-scale SPARQL to Cypher translation. Unlike existing solutions, S2CLite is purely rule-based (inspired by traditional programming language compilers) and operates without requiring an RDF graph or external tools.\nL17: Experiments conducted on the BSBM42 and Spider4SPARQL datasets show that S2CLite significantly reduces query parsing errors, achieving a total parsing accuracy of 77.8% on Spider4SPARQL compared to 44.2% by the state-of-the-art S2CTrans. Furthermore, S2CLite achieved a 96.6\\% execution accuracy on the intersecting subset of queries parsed by both parsers, outperforming S2CTrans by 7.3%.\nL18: We further use S2CLite to parse Spider4SPARQL queries to Cypher and generate Spider4SSC, a unified Text-to-Query language (SQL, SPARQL, Cypher) dataset with 4525 unique questions and 3 equivalent sets of 2581 matching queries (SQL, SPARQL and Cypher). We open-source S2CLite for further development on GitHub (cite9†this http URL†github.com ) and provide the clean Spider4SSC dataset for download.\nL19: Subjects:  | Computation and Language (cs.CL)\nL20: Cite as:  | cite10†arXiv:2511.09354 [cs.CL]\nL21:    | (or cite11†arXiv:2511.09354v1 [cs.CL] for this version)\nL22:    | cite12†https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2511.09354†doi.org arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\nL23: ## Submission history\nL24: \nL25: From: Martin Vejvar [cite13†view email ]\nL26: [v1] Wed, 12 Nov 2025 14:12:11 UTC (8,842 KB)\nL27: \nL28: Full-text links:\nL29: \nL30: ## Access Paper:\nL31: \nL32: View a PDF of the paper titled Spider4SSC & S2CLite: A text-to-multi-query-language dataset using lightweight ontology-agnostic SPARQL to Cypher parser, by Martin Vejvar and Yasutaka Fujimoto\nL33: \nL34:   * cite7†View PDF L35:   * cite8†HTML (experimental) L36:   * cite14†TeX Source L37: \nL38: cite15†view license†creativecommons.org L39: ### Current browse context:\nL40: \nL41: cs.CL\nL42: \nL43: cite16†< prev |   cite17†next > L44: \nL45: cite18†new | cite19†recent | cite20†2025-11 L46: \nL47: Change to browse by:\nL48: \nL49: cite21†cs L50: \nL51: ### References & Citations\nL52: \nL53:   * cite22†NASA ADS†ui.adsabs.harvard.edu L54:   * cite23†Google Scholar†scholar.google.com L55:   * cite24†Semantic Scholar†api.semanticscholar.org L56: \nL57: [Button: export BibTeX citation] Loading...\nL58: \nL59: ## BibTeX formatted citation\nL60: \nL61: [Button: ×]\nL62: \nL63: loading...\nL64: \nL65: Data provided by:\nL66: \nL67: ### Bookmark\nL68: \nL69: [Input] Bibliographic Tools\nL70: # Bibliographic and Citation Tools\nL71: \nL72: [Input] Bibliographic Explorer Toggle\n